e in practice it does seem like  the rule is not going to affect Bernie  in 2020 if he runs because there's this  interesting thing that Vermont has done  at the state level  not passed a resolution recently which  says doesn't matter whether Bernie runs  as a Democrat or as an independent for  his Senate seat not for president but  for his Senate seat  Vermont passed a resolution which says  quote for all purposes and entitled to  all the rights and privileges that come  with such membership at the state and  federal level and what this means  assuming that the state resolution holds  is that Vermont is basically saying  Bernie Sanders is a Democrat and thus  the DNC wouldn't have a case to keep him  out of the primary in 2020 if Bernie  Sanders chooses to run I actually come  down somewhere else on this I've quite  frankly don't care whether or not Bernie  has a way around the rule the rule is  still the rule and it would affect the  Bernie Sanders were it not for the  Vermont resolution being passed and the  fact that Bernie Sanders has a way  around the rule doesn't mean we should  pretend for a second that this was a  logical thing for the DNC to do I don't  deny that there is a legitimate debate  that the DNC can have who's allowed to  run in the primary do you have to be a  registered Democrat you have to caucus  with Democrats if so for how long that's  a totally reasonable conversation to  have but to go through the 2016 election  debacle and to so clearly need to  regroup and rethink your strategy and  think of the best way to fight Trump and  to come away passing this rule as much  as lack of voter enthusiasm scares me  for Democrats in 2018 this stuff also  scares me for 2018 and I said from day  one that Tom Perez as DNC chair was a  disaster I said from day one that if the  Democratic Party wants to figure out  what to do they need to not do what  they've historically done which is to  just ignore the actual mistakes that  have been made there is a contingent  that is saying it's dishonest to claim  that this is all about Bernie I already  told you that whether or not there is an  actual question about the super delegate  process and eliminating super-delegates  being facilitated by passing this law  this for me is part of what the DNC has  done wrong since 2016 leadership roles  going to Clinton surrogates the 2016  fiasco not even really being addressed  in a serious manner and if your argument  is we should give the benefit of the  doubt to the DNC for this move and just  accept that they want to do what Bernie  wants which is to get rid of  super-delegates  what is the Democratic Party done to  earn the benefit of the doubt since 2016  I can't think of anything and I'm asked  I'm not asking rhetorically I'm actually  asking tell me what the DNC has done  since 2016 that warrants me giving them  the benefit of the doubt on this I can't  think of anything period
 

